// Rejection: an empty `0x` literal (no digits after the prefix) used to lex as
// a 0-valued INT, pass `wyn check`, and then hit an internal codegen error. It
// must be a clean LEXER error at check time. Valid radix literals are covered
// by tests/regression/radix_literal_ok.wyn.
//
// Why the check-phase directive and not the either-phase one: "at check time" is
// the whole point of the rule, and only the check-phase directive asserts which
// phase rejected. With the either-phase directive the mutant that deletes the
// lexer guard still exits non-zero - the C backend emits a raw clang error,
// "invalid suffix 'x' on integer constant" - so the gate would stay green on
// exactly the regression it exists to catch. Measured, not assumed.
//
// Mirrors arm 1 of 2 of tests/errors/run_empty_radix_literal_test.sh, which is
// deliberately RETAINED: .github/workflows/ci.yml gates that script on Windows
// and excludes run_bdd.sh there, so deleting it would DROP Windows coverage of
// this rule rather than move it. Retire it in a follow-up once the rejection
// path here is Windows-runnable.
// EXPECT_CHECK_FAIL:
// EXPECT_ERR: hex literal '0x' needs at least one digit
fn main() {
    var a = 0x
    println("${a}")
}
